 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-The method adds a convolution step to the training loop. The complexity is $O(N \cdot K^2)$ per image per iteration, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $K$ is the kernel size. Since $K$ is small (e.g., $9 \times 9$) and decreases over time, the overhead is minimal compared to the rendering cost [S4]. Memory usage increases by the storage of the blurred image buffer [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time:** The convolution operation is $O(N \cdot K^2)$ per image, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $K$ is the kernel size. However, using separable kernels reduces this to $O(N \cdot K)$ [S1]. The schedule updates are negligible $O(1)$ per iteration.
+*   **Memory:** Requires storing the blurred image or recomputing it on the fly. No additional large data structures are needed beyond the kernel [S1].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-On the DTU 3-view dataset, Smoothed 2D Supervision (as part of Adaptive Gaussian Blur) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the baseline of 19.54 PSNR [S3]. It reduces training iterations to 50k compared to 200k for previous MLP-based methods, reducing overall training time to 25% [S1].
-
-**Scaling:**
-The method scales well with image resolution as the convolution is local. However, the kernel size must be adjusted for downsampling to maintain effective frequency control [S4].
+*   On DTU 3-view, Smoothed 2D Supervision (specifically the "Adaptive Gaussian Blur" variant) achieves 19.72 PSNR, improving over the Baseline of 19.54 PSNR [S3].
+*   It is experimentally proven crucial for successful pose refinement in ablation studies [S1].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment, restricts high-frequency noise, and reduces training time [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** It is handcrafted and cannot fully align frequency relationships between positional encodings and pixel supervision as effectively as adaptive methods [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Stabilizes camera alignment and joint optimization [S2, unofficial]. Reduces training iterations required (e.g., 50k vs 200k in some MLP-based methods) by providing robust gradients [S1]. Restricts the spectrum of the learned scene to prevent overfitting to noise [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** The strategies (linear or adaptive Gaussian blur) are handcrafted and cannot fully align frequency relationships between positional encodings and pixel supervision as effectively as adaptive methods [S3].
 
 **When to Use:**
-Use when joint optimization of camera poses and scene parameters is unstable, or when training time needs to be reduced [S1]. It is particularly useful in ablation studies for pose refinement [S1].
+*   Use when joint optimization of camera poses and scene representation is unstable [S1].
+*   Use when training requires stabilization in the early phases to avoid local minima [S2, unofficial].
 
 **When Not to Use:**
-Avoid if maximum possible PSNR is required and adaptive frequency alignment is available, as handcrafted blur may not fully align with the model's frequency biases [S3].
+*   When high-frequency detail preservation is critical from the start and the overhead of managing the schedule is not justified [S3].
+*   When using an adaptive frequency regularization method that already handles spectral alignment [S3].
 
 ## Background
-Smoothed 2D Supervision was proposed as part of techniques to improve robustness in joint optimization of camera pose and decomposed low-rank tensorial radiance fields [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was inspired by spectral analysis showing that blurring 2D training images produces smoothed image gradients and stabilizes camera alignment [S2, unofficial]. The method is also referenced in the context of Trick-GS, where it is used to balance training efficiency and quality [S4].
+Smoothed 2D Supervision originated as a technique to improve the robustness of joint optimization in radiance fields and Gaussian Splatting [S1][S2, unofficial]. It was inspired by spectral analysis showing that blurring training images produces smoother gradients and stabilizes camera alignment [S2, unofficial]. The method is closely related to the Gaussian schedules used in 3D radiance fields [S2, unofficial]. It has been adopted in various forms, including "Adaptive Gaussian Blur" in few-shot NeRF [S3] and "Trick-GS" in efficient Gaussian Splatting [S4].
 
